Compiler vs Interpreter
Compiler: Converts the entire source code into machine code or bytecode before execution.
Interpreter: Executes code line by line.

In Java:

Java source code is compiled by the Java Compiler into bytecode.
The bytecode is then executed by the Java Virtual Machine (JVM), which acts like an interpreter for bytecode.

What is JVM?
JVM stands for Java Virtual Machine.
It is the runtime environment that:

Executes Java bytecode
Provides platform independence
Handles memory management and security

What is JRE?
JRE stands for Java Runtime Environment.
It contains:

JVM
Core libraries
Runtime components
It is used to run Java programs.

What is JDK?
JDK stands for Java Development Kit.
It contains:

JRE
Compiler (javac)
Debugger
Development tools
It is used to develop Java programs.

Platform Independence
Java is called platform-independent because Java code is compiled once into bytecode and can run on any machine that has a JVM.

This is the main reason Java became popular.

Bytecode
Bytecode is an intermediate code generated by the Java compiler.
It is not machine-specific, so it can run on any platform with JVM support.

Java Program Execution Flow
Source code (.java) → compiled by javac → bytecode (.class) → executed by JVM